This paper sits between the low-scoring papers (1-3, which have fundamental flaws) and the medium-scoring normalization studies (4-6). It has genuine contributions (large-effect findings on position, discovery of heterogeneous benefits, useful taxonomy) but is held back by the absence of variance reporting in an empirical study and a thin validation experiment. It is comparable to the old architecture ablation paper (5.0) and the DyT paper (4.25), perhaps slightly above DyT due to clearer findings but slightly below the ablation paper due to the no-variance issue. I place it at **4.5**.

## Score and Decision

**Originality:** Moderate — the study is systematic and well-scoped, but the individual findings (post-normalization helps, different norms can complement) are not deeply surprising given existing Transformer normalization literature. The taxonomy is a useful organizational contribution.

**Importance of research question:** Moderate-to-high — normalization design in Mamba variants is currently ad hoc, and a systematic study addresses a real gap.

**Claims well supported:** Partially — the large-effect findings (position) are well supported, but ranking of close configurations and the validation improvement (0.3% vision) are not convincingly established without variance.

**Soundness of experiments:** Weakened by no variance reporting and thin validation; the core ablation design is sound.

**Clarity:** Adequate but with confusing errors in the validation section text.

**Value to community:** Moderate — practitioners will find the tables and taxonomy useful even if the analysis is shallow.

