DiffSDA is a genuinely novel contribution (first diffusion-based sequential disentanglement) with impressive practical results, but the evaluation methodology on visual data has significant gaps: preservation metrics don't measure factor independence, the reconstruction comparison is confounded by the pre-trained VQ-VAE, and no ablations validate the architectural claims. The audio results on TIMIT with proper metrics (EER) are more convincing and show real improvement (42.29% disentanglement gap vs. 31.11%), but the visual evaluation — the paper's primary focus — remains under-supported. This places the paper above the low-scoring anchors (which had overclaimed disentanglement with no proper metrics at all) but below the high-scoring anchors (which had rigorous evaluation). It sits in the borderline range, leaning toward acceptance due to genuine novelty and practical value.

