The paper reveals a striking disconnect between solution accuracy and parameter sensitivity accuracy in neural operators that has significant practical implications: a surrogate model that predicts solutions with R²=0.997 can simultaneously have Jacobian R²=0.036. This suggests that the neural operator community's typical evaluation (solution MSE only) may be systematically missing a critical failure mode, particularly for any downstream task that depends on how the model uses its inputs (inversion, optimization, sensitivity analysis). The identical R² values in the inversion table, if a reporting error, ironically highlight how much the community needs better per-parameter diagnostic evaluation.

