##Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Novel and practical threat model addressing multi-model multi-task black-box attacks with few queries**: Prior multi-task adversarial attacks assume white-box access to shared layers in a single model. This paper explicitly targets multi-model multi-task systems (separate models per each task) with only black-box API feedback and 100 queries, formalized in Section 3 with three victim models including commercial APIs (Baidu and Ali Translate). This is a first work to addresses RQ1–RQ4 simultaneously.

2. **Effective cluster-based reduction of multi-task to single-task attack**: The key mechanism—concatenating embeddings of inputs and multi-task outputs, clustering them into pseudo-labels, and training substitute models classifiers (Algorithm 1, lines 1–9)—is a creative and principled way to sidestep the complexity of multi-task attacks. This is a well-supported empirically: Table 1 shows CEMA outperforms all baselines by large margins across both classification ( e.g., 73.57% vs. 46.11% on dis-sst5) and 62.27% vs. 41.90% on dis-emotion) and and translation (e.g., BLEU 0.14 vs. 0.24 on opus-mt).

