### Harsh Critic
# Review of RMP-SAM: Towards Real-Time Multi-Purpose Segment Anything

## Critical Issues

### 1. Unfair Baseline Comparisons Undermine the "Best Trade-off" Claim [Structural]

The central claim that RMP-SAM achieves "the best speed and accuracy trade-off" rests on comparisons in Table 3, where Mask2Former, MaskFormer, kMaX-DeepLab, and YOSO are extended to the multi-purpose setting. These methods were never designed for joint panoptic + video instance + interactive segmentation. The paper states they are "re-implemented using the same codebase" but provides no detail on how each baseline was adapted—what architectural modifications were made, how queries were extended for video and interactive tasks, or whether training strategies were tuned for multi-task learning. A method designed for a setting will naturally outperform methods naively extended to it. Without demonstrating that baselines received comparable engineering effort and task-specific tuning, the headline result is unreliable. This is structural: the benchmark itself does not support the claim drawn from it.

### 2. Joint Co-Training Degrades Individual Task Performance [Evidential]

Table 6(b) reveals that multi-task learning *hurts* the primary task: COCO-Panoptic PQ drops from 36.6 (single-task) to 36.2 (+video) to 35.7 (+video+interactive). YouTube-VIS mAP drops from 36.0 to 35.3 when interactive segmentation is added. The paper acknowledges this briefly ("Adding COCO-SAM training leads to a few performance drops") but does not reconcile it with the claim of achieving "the best accuracy and speed trade-off on all three tasks." If the multi-purpose model is meaningfully worse at each individual task than a dedicated model, the value proposition of the unified model requires more careful justification than the paper provides. The claim that RMP-SAM generalizes well is not supported when the model's own ablation shows degradation.

### 3. "Real-Time" Measured Only on A100 [Evidential]

All FPS measurements are taken on a single A100 GPU (Sec. 4). An A100 is a data-center GPU with 80GB HBM2e; "real-time" in the paper's title and framing implies deployment on resource-constrained or consumer devices. A model running at 35 FPS on an A100 may run at 5–10 FPS on an edge GPU or mobile NPU, at which point it is no longer real-time. The paper makes no attempt to test on realistic deployment hardware, nor does it acknowledge this gap. The TopFormer variant achieves only 30.7 FPS on A100—barely real-time even on this hardware—yet is presented as a real-time solution.

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract & Introduction**: The paper claims to be the "first" real-time multi-purpose segmentation model. This is plausible but depends on how the setting is defined—SAM-2 also unifies image and video interactive segmentation and is mentioned in Table 1 but never experimentally compared against. The omission of SAM-2 from experiments is notable given its direct relevance.

**Section 2 (Problem Settings)**: The decision to exclude SAM training data from the primary benchmark is reasonable but should be more clearly justified. The argument that "SAM data has no semantics" applies to the interactive segmentation sub-task as well, yet the model is still evaluated on interactive segmentation using COCO-derived prompts. The evaluation protocol for interactive segmentation at test time uses the *center point of the ground truth mask* as the prompt—an artificially easy setting that does not reflect real interactive use where users click arbitrary locations.

**Section 3 (Method)**: The dynamic convolution design (Eq. 3–5) is directly adopted from prior work (Zhang et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2021; Li et al., 2022c), which the paper acknowledges. The main architectural novelty is the asymmetric adapter (DC for object queries, cross-attention for prompt queries). This is a reasonable but incremental design choice. The CLIP text embedding strategy for unifying label taxonomies is mentioned in passing but never ablated—it could be a significant factor in the cross-dataset results.

**Section 4 (Experiments)**: Table 4 has an inconsistency: the text says boxes are generated using "Mask R-CNN with ResNet50" but the table caption says "Faster R-CNN." This should be clarified, as the detector quality directly affects the upper bound of interactive segmentation performance. Table 6(a) shows that the decoupled decoder + decoupled adapter (Fig. 3d) achieves the best PQ (45.2), yet the paper selects the shared decoder + decoupled adapter (Fig. 3c, PQ 44.6) as the final design—presumably for speed, but **no FPS numbers are reported for these ablation variants**. Without this, the claim that the chosen design achieves the "best speed and accuracy trade-off" is unsupported by the paper's own ablation.

**Table 3**: Mask2Former-R18 achieves PQ=35.6 with 31.2 FPS while RMP-SAM-R18 achieves PQ=39.9 with 40.3 FPS. The 4.3 PQ gap is substantial, but it is unclear whether Mask2Former's multi-task extension received the same adapter/query design considerations as RMP-SAM, or was simply given extra query slots.

## Strengths

1. The problem setting—unifying panoptic, video instance, and interactive segmentation under a real-time constraint—is practically motivated and well-defined. Table 1 clearly delineates the capability gaps of prior work.

2. The asymmetric adapter insight (DC for object queries needing scene context, cross-attention for prompt queries needing local detail) is well-motivated by the distinct goals of each query type, and the ablation in Table 6(d) provides clean evidence for this design.

3. The meta-architecture exploration (Fig. 3, Table 6a) is a useful contribution for future work on multi-task decoder design, even though the chosen variant isn't the strongest performer.

4. The benchmark construction—extending existing methods to the multi-purpose setting and training under identical conditions—provides a starting point for the community, even if the baseline fairness is questionable.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **SAM-2 comparison**: SAM-2 unifies image and video interactive segmentation and is directly relevant. Without comparing against it, the claim of superiority in the multi-purpose setting is incomplete.
2. **FPS measurements on deployment hardware**: Test on at least one consumer/edge GPU (e.g., T4, Jetson) to substantiate the "real-time" claim for realistic deployment scenarios.
3. **FPS in ablation studies**: Table 6(a) meta-architecture variants lack FPS data. Without this, the trade-off analysis is incomplete and the architecture selection is unjustified.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Analysis of multi-task interference**: Table 6(b) shows performance degradation with co-training. The paper needs to analyze *why* and *where* the interference occurs—does it come from query competition, gradient conflicts, or label space incompatibility? This would strengthen the contribution beyond "we observe a drop."
2. **Interactive segmentation under realistic prompts**: Evaluate with random point clicks (not just center points) and with multi-point iterative refinement, as SAM is typically evaluated.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Failure cases**: No analysis of when the unified model fails compared to task-specific models. This would reveal whether the multi-task setting introduces systematic failure modes.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Report per-task performance of dedicated single-task models** alongside the multi-purpose results in Table 3, so readers can directly assess the cost of unification.

## Overall Assessment

The paper identifies a practically relevant problem setting and proposes a reasonable architecture, but the central claims are undermined by unfair baseline comparisons, evidence that multi-task learning degrades individual task performance, and a "real-time" designation based solely on A100 measurements. The asymmetric adapter is a genuine insight supported by clean ablation, but the surrounding evaluation framework does not establish that RMP-SAM achieves the "best speed and accuracy trade-off" in any rigorous sense. The benchmark contributions are useful starting points but need fairer baselines and more transparent adaptation details. The paper should not be accepted in its current form; the evaluation needs to be substantially strengthened—particularly through fairer baselines, deployment-hardware speed tests, and honest reporting of the multi-task performance cost.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Superior accuracy-speed trade-off on the proposed multi-task benchmark**: Table 3 demonstrates RMP-SAM consistently achieves the best balance across all three tasks and all backbones. For example, with R18, RMP-SAM attains 39.9 PQ / 34.8 mIoU / 38.7 mAP at 40.3 FPS, whereas Mask2Former-R18 achieves 35.6 PQ / 30.3 mIoU / 34.1 mAP at only 31.2 FPS. With R50, the gap widens: 46.9 PQ at 35.1 FPS vs. 42.9 PQ at 26.6 FPS.

2. **Effective pooling-based dynamic convolution replacing per-pixel cross-attention**: The core decoder design replaces expensive pixel-wise cross-attention with pooling-based dynamic convolution with gating (DCG). Table 6(c) shows this matches or exceeds per-pixel cross-attention performance (44.6 PQ, 56.7 COCO-SAM vs. 45.0 PQ, 55.3 COCO-SAM) while enabling real-time speeds by avoiding the quadratic cost of pixel-wise attention.

3. **Asymmetric adapter design is well-motivated and empirically validated**: The insight that object queries (panoptic/video) need scene-level context while prompt queries (interactive) need local detail drives the asymmetric choice: DC for A_obj and CA for A_prompt. Table 6(d) confirms this—DC+CA achieves 44.6 PQ / 56.7 COCO-SAM, notably outperforming symmetric DC+DC (44.7 / 52.1) and CA+CA (42.6 / 54.3), demonstrating that the asymmetric design resolves the conflict between the two query types.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Novel and well-defined problem setting**: Tables 1 and 2 systematically characterize the "real-time multi-purpose segmentation" setting, showing no prior method simultaneously supports image masks, video masks, interactive segmentation, semantic labels, and multi-task capability in a real-time framework. This formalization is a meaningful contribution to the community.

2. **Generalization beyond the benchmark**: Table 5(a) shows RMP-SAM-R18 achieves 32.5 VPQ at 30 FPS on VIP-Seg video panoptic segmentation, beating Tube-Link (STDCv2) at 31.4 VPQ / 12 FPS despite not being specifically designed for VPS. Table 5(b) shows competitive ADE-20K results (38.3 PQ with R50).

3. **Multi-purpose advantage over SAM-like methods**: Table 4 shows RMP-SAM achieves 33.2 mAP on COCO instance segmentation with 35G FLOPs—competitive with TinySAM (32.5), MobileSAM (32.1), and EdgeSAM (31.9)—while being the only method that also supports panoptic and video instance segmentation, adding practical utility beyond what any single-purpose efficient SAM variant offers.

4. **Thorough meta-architecture ablation**: Table 6(a) evaluates four meta-architectures (Fig. 3a–d), revealing that shared decoder + decoupled adapter (Fig. 3c) offers the best parameter-performance trade-off (47.3M params, 44.6 PQ / 56.7 COCO-SAM). The finding that decoupled decoders do not help under lightweight backbones contrasts with prior multi-task work and provides useful design guidance.

### Presentation / practical strengths
1. **Comprehensive and fair benchmarking**: The authors re-implement Mask2Former, MaskFormer, kMaX-DeepLab, and YOSO under identical training settings (same codebase, 12 epochs, same augmentation, same hardware for speed testing), making the benchmark reliable and reproducible.

## Summary

The paper introduces the novel "real-time multi-purpose segmentation" setting—unifying interactive, panoptic, and video instance segmentation in a single real-time model—and proposes RMP-SAM, which uses a pooling-based dynamic convolution decoder with asymmetric adapters (DC for object queries, CA for prompt queries). The single most important piece of evidence is Table 3, where RMP-SAM achieves the best accuracy-speed trade-off across all three tasks and all backbone choices (e.g., 46.9 PQ / 39.8 mIoU / 46.2 mAP at 35.1 FPS with R50), substantially outperforming prior baselines like Mask2Former (42.9 PQ / 35.6 mIoU / 42.1 mAP at 26.6 FPS) that cannot match both accuracy and speed simultaneously.

