### Harsh Critic
# Review: Conformal Confidence Sets for Biomedical Image Segmentation

## Critical Issues

### 1. Lack of meaningful baselines for efficiency comparison (Evidential)

The paper's central practical claim is that distance-transformed scores produce "tight" confidence sets. Yet the only efficiency comparisons are against (a) raw logit scores, which the paper itself demonstrates produce uninformative outer sets (Figures 1, 5, A18), and (b) bounding box methods, which target a fundamentally different object (a box around the mask rather than the mask itself) and are inherently conservative. Comparing favorably to these straw men does not establish that the resulting sets are actually tight in any meaningful sense. There is no comparison to any mask-level conformal method that could serve as a competitive baseline—such as pixel-wise conformal prediction with Bonferroni/FWER correction, or the risk-control approach of Angelopoulos et al. (2024) applied at a mask level rather than an FDR level. Without such comparisons, the claims about "tight" and "informative" sets are unsupported. This affects Sections 3.4, 4, 5, and the abstract.

### 2. The theoretical contribution is a straightforward application of existing conformal prediction (Structural)

Theorems 2.1, 2.2, and 2.6 follow directly from standard split conformal inference once the nonconformity scores (max of transformed logits inside/outside the mask) are defined. The paper acknowledges this connection explicitly in Remark 2.4, noting that inner/outer coverage "can also be viewed as a special case of conformal risk control." The only genuinely novel theoretical result is Theorem 2.8, which links Hausdorff distance of the predicted mask to tightness of confidence sets under distance transformations. But this theorem is conditional on model accuracy—it says that if your model is good, your sets are tight—which limits its practical value (uncertainty quantification is most needed when the model is poor). The paper frames the theoretical framework as a central contribution, but the conformal guarantees are essentially free once you choose a score function within the standard split-conformal setup.

### 3. Evaluation is primarily qualitative; quantitative characterization of set sizes is inadequate (Evidential)

For a method whose selling point is "tight" confidence sets, the paper provides remarkably little quantitative characterization of set sizes. Figure 5 shows diameter ratios (a coarse metric that collapses a 2D region to a single scalar), but even this is only for the polyps dataset. The brain and teeth sections (Sections 4, 5) contain only visual examples and references to appendix coverage validation—no systematic comparison of set sizes across transformations, coverage levels, or methods. Key quantities like the average fraction of the image covered by outer sets, or the average area of the symmetric difference between inner/outer sets and the ground truth, are absent. This makes it impossible to assess whether the sets are tight enough to be clinically useful or merely less loose than trivial baselines. This affects Sections 3.4, 4, 5.

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract:** Claims the method provides "tight inner and outer confidence sets" while controlling the false coverage rate. The tightness claim is not well-supported by the evidence presented.

**Section 2.2:** The notation $[(1-\alpha)(n+1)]$ in equations (3) and (5) is ambiguous—does this mean floor, ceiling, or rounding? The standard conformal prediction literature uses $\lceil(1-\alpha)(n+1)\rceil/n$, and the exact choice affects finite-sample coverage. This should be clarified.

**Section 2.4:** The paper recommends using training data as part of the learning dataset for choosing transformations, then acknowledges that "machine learning models typically overfit their training data meaning that certain score functions may appear to perform better on this data than they do in practice." This is a real concern that is dismissed too quickly. In the teeth application (Section 5), the training data *is* used as the learning dataset, meaning the transformation is selected based on data the model has already seen. This could systematically favor transformations that look good on overfit data but generalize poorly.

**Section 2.5:** The discussion of bounding box methods and their exchangeability violation (from de Grancey et al., 2022) is interesting but the resolution—treating the union of boxes as the ground truth—means the comparison in Section 3 is against a method that is already known to be conservative by construction.

**Section 3.1:** The learning dataset of 298 images is used to both choose transformations AND to calibrate thresholds (the thresholds shown in Figure 1 are "obtained from calibrating over the learning dataset"). Then Section 3.2 uses a separate calibration dataset. This is fine methodologically, but the visual evidence in Figures 1-2 uses the learning dataset for both purposes simultaneously, which could make the transformations appear more effective than they are on independent data.

**Section 3.3:** The coverage validation divides the same 1500 images into 1000/500 splits 1000 times. The resulting coverage estimates are not independent across runs (images are shared across splits), so the uncertainty bands in Figure 4 are narrower than they should be. This isn't a validity concern—the coverage guarantee holds regardless—but it overstates the precision of the empirical coverage estimates.

**Section 3.4:** The diameter ratio metric conflates sets with very different shapes. A thin annular ring and a compact blob could have similar diameter ratios but very different areas relative to the ground truth. This limits what can be concluded from Figure 5.

**Sections 4 and 5:** These sections are remarkably thin for claiming contributions on three applications. Each contains roughly one paragraph of methodological description and one figure. The brain section (Section 4) refers almost entirely to the appendix, and the teeth section (Section 5) does the same. The paper would be stronger with fewer, more thoroughly evaluated applications.

## Strengths

1. The identification of distance transformations as effective score functions for outer confidence sets is a practically valuable insight. The visual evidence (Figures 1, 2, A18) clearly demonstrates that raw logits produce uninformative outer sets, and the distance transformation addresses this failure mode convincingly.

2. The theoretical framework is clean and correct. The formalization of inner/outer confidence sets with explicit coverage guarantees (Theorems 2.1, 2.2, 2.6) and the joint coverage result (Corollary 2.5) are clearly presented, even if they follow from standard conformal inference.

3. The connection to Hausdorff distance (Theorem 2.8) provides a principled justification for why distance-transformed scores should be preferred: they convert segmentation accuracy directly into confidence set tightness. This is a meaningful theoretical insight even though it is conditional on model quality.

4. The paper is honest about limitations—the discussion of when logit scores fail (brain imaging, Figure A18), the acknowledgment that poor models yield poor confidence sets, and the explicit discussion of the tradeoff between precision and coverage level.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments

1. **Comparison to pixel-wise conformal prediction with FWER control.** This is the most natural baseline for mask-level coverage guarantees (the stated goal of the paper). Without this comparison, the claim that distance-transformed scores produce "tight" sets relative to what is achievable under the same coverage criterion is unsupported.

2. **Quantitative comparison of set sizes across all three applications.** Report average area of inner sets, outer sets, and symmetric differences with ground truth across coverage levels, not just diameter ratios for one dataset.

3. **Evaluation of failure cases.** Show what happens when the segmentation model misses a polyp entirely or makes a gross error. The method's value for uncertainty quantification is most important in these cases.

### Deeper Analysis Needed

1. **Sensitivity to learning dataset composition and size.** The paper recommends using a learning dataset but provides no analysis of how performance degrades as the learning dataset shrinks, or how the choice of transformation varies across random learning dataset splits.

2. **Analysis of the conditional (per-image) coverage rather than marginal coverage.** Marginal coverage at 90% could mask systematic over-coverage on easy images and under-coverage on hard ones. The medical imaging setting demands reliability across image difficulty levels.

### Visualizations & Case Studies

1. **Quantitative summary tables** of set sizes (area ratio, symmetric difference, Hausdorff distance of confidence sets to ground truth) across transformations, coverage levels, and datasets. The current evaluation relies too heavily on cherry-picked visual examples.

### Obvious Next Steps

1. **Condense to two well-evaluated applications** rather than three superficially evaluated ones. The polyps and brain datasets show contrasting behavior (logits work for inner sets in polyps but fail for brain), making them the most informative pair.

## Overall Assessment

The paper identifies a genuinely useful insight—that distance transformations of logit scores produce much tighter outer confidence sets than raw logits for conformal image segmentation—and provides a clean theoretical wrapper around it. However, the theoretical contribution is largely a straightforward application of split conformal inference, and the evaluation does not convincingly support the central claim of "tight" confidence sets. The only efficiency comparisons are against approaches that are either known to produce poor outer sets (raw logits) or target a different object entirely (bounding boxes). Without comparison to a competitive mask-level conformal baseline, and without quantitative characterization of set sizes beyond diameter ratios on one dataset, the practical significance of the method remains unclear. The paper should not be accepted in its current form; it needs either substantially stronger experimental evaluation (competitive baselines, quantitative set-size analysis across all datasets) or a reframing of claims to match what the evidence actually establishes—that distance transformations improve upon naive logit thresholds, with untested efficiency relative to other mask-level conformal approaches.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Probabilistic coverage guarantees for mask containment, not just FDR control**: Unlike prior conformal image segmentation work (Bates et al., 2021; Angelopoulos et al., 2024) that controls the expected proportion of false negatives, this paper provides guarantees that the true mask is entirely contained within/contains the confidence set with a specified probability. Theorems 2.1 and 2.2 establish these inner and outer coverage guarantees (equations 4 and 6) under standard exchangeability assumptions, which is a strictly stronger and more clinically meaningful error criterion as argued in Section 1.

2. **Distance transformation of scores dramatically tightens outer confidence sets, with formal justification**: The key practical insight is that naive logit scores yield very loose outer sets, while distance-transformed scores produce tight bounds. This is empirically demonstrated in Figure 1 (histograms showing DT scores achieve far better separation of inside/outside distributions at the outer threshold), Figure 5 (efficiency ratios showing DT scores closest to 1 for outer sets), and Figure A20 (brain imaging where logit-based sets are uninformative but DT-based sets are tight). Theorem 2.8 provides formal justification: when distance-transformed scores are used, accurate segmentation (small Hausdorff distance ≤ k on a sufficient fraction of calibration data) guarantees the outer set's Hausdorff distance from the prediction is ≤ k, and from the truth is ≤ 2k—a relationship the paper explicitly notes does not hold for untransformed logit scores.

3. **Validated coverage across three diverse biomedical applications with 1000-run Monte Carlo**: The method is applied to polyps segmentation (PraNet, 1798 images), brain imaging (HD-BET, 524 images), and teeth segmentation (U-Net GAN, 598 subjects). Figure 4 shows coverage rates at or above nominal levels across all confidence levels for all score transformations, with 95% uncertainty bands from 1000 random calibration/test splits, confirming the theoretical guarantees hold in practice.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Joint confidence sets with flexible alpha allocation**: Corollary 2.5 allows asymmetric spending of the error budget (α₁ + α₂ ≤ α) between inner and outer sets, which is demonstrated to be useful in the polyps application where identifying where the polyp ends is harder (Section 3.1 chooses α₁ = 0.02, α₂ = 0.08). Theorem 2.6 offers an alternative joint approach using the joint distribution of maxima. Remark 2.7 discusses the tradeoffs between these approaches.

2. **Learning dataset framework for transformation selection without compromising validity**: Section 2.4 explicitly recommends setting aside an independent learning dataset to choose score transformations, and demonstrates that this is crucial—for instance, in the teeth application (Section 5), the learning dataset revealed that smoothed scores work best for inner sets while distance-transformed scores work best for outer sets. The independence of this dataset from calibration data preserves the exchangeability assumption.

3. **Prior bounding box methods shown as a special case**: The paper establishes in Appendix A.4 (Corollaries A.6 and A.7) that the image-wise max-additive method of Andéol et al. (2023) is a special case of their framework using chessboard distance transformation on bounding boxes, and Figure 5 shows their distance transformation on masks produces tighter confidence sets than the bounding box approach.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Computational efficiency**: The method requires only ~0.03 seconds (downscaled) or ~2.64 seconds (original) to compute calibration thresholds over 1000 images on a standard laptop, making it immediately practical for clinical use.

2. **Algorithm 1 provides clear test-time implementation**: The paper provides an explicit algorithm for computing confidence sets at test time, facilitating reproducibility.

## Summary

The paper develops conformal confidence sets that provide probabilistic guarantees (not just FDR control) that the true segmentation mask is contained within inner and outer bounds. The most important piece of evidence that this works is the combination of Theorem 2.8—which formally establishes that distance-transformed scores translate segmentation accuracy into tight confidence bounds (a property untransformed logits lack)—and the empirical validation in Figure 5, which shows distance-transformed scores achieve efficiency ratios (diameter of confidence set / diameter of true mask) much closer to 1 for outer sets than both logit scores and bounding box scores across all confidence levels on the polyps dataset, while Figure 4 confirms coverage is maintained at the nominal level across 1000 random splits.

