### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

### 1. Fundamentally unfair baseline comparison (Structural)

The paper's headline claim is that DrugAgent "significantly outperformed GPT-4 across multiple evaluation metrics." GPT-4 is a general-purpose language model, not a DTI prediction system. Asking GPT-4 to predict binding affinities zero-shot and comparing it to a system that wraps a domain-specific ML model (DeepPurpose, trained on BindingDB) is not a meaningful comparison—it is a straw man. This invalidates the central empirical claim of the paper. The relevant baselines are specialized DTI prediction methods (DeepDTA, GraphDTA, etc.) and, critically, the AI agent alone (DeepPurpose without the multi-agent wrapper). The paper includes neither comparison. (Sections 4.1, Table 1, Abstract)

### 2. The ablation study does not test the multi-agent contribution (Structural)

The ablation removing the AI Agent (w.o. AI Agent, MSE=52.349) merely confirms that removing the only binding-affinity predictor destroys performance—this is trivially expected. The ablation that matters for validating the multi-agent framework is the reverse: how does the AI agent alone (DeepPurpose) compare to the full multi-agent system? This comparison is absent. The closest proxy is "w.o. Search Agent" (MSE=1.960 vs. full system 1.836), which shows the Search Agent contributes almost nothing. The "w.o. KG Agent" condition (MSE=8.119) shows a larger gap, but without the AI-agent-only baseline, we cannot determine whether the KG agent adds value beyond what DeepPurpose already provides. The ablation as presented cannot support the claim that the multi-agent architecture is beneficial. (Section 4.1, Table 1)

### 3. Evaluation on only 10 drug-target pairs (Evidential)

All quantitative results in Table 1 are computed on just 10 drug-target pairs. Standard DTI benchmarks evaluate on hundreds to thousands of pairs. With N=10, individual outliers dominate aggregate metrics, standard deviations are unreliable estimates of true variance, and no meaningful statistical conclusions can be drawn. The reported standard deviations are suspiciously small (e.g., MSE std of 0.007), suggesting these may be over the 5 random runs rather than over the data distribution, which further undermines their interpretability. The paper lacks any justification for this sample size or analysis of its adequacy. (Section 4.1, Table 1)

### 4. Scale incompatibility in score merging (Methodological gap)

The three agents produce scores on incommensurable scales: the AI agent outputs continuous binding affinity predictions (pKd, typically ~5–12), the KG agent outputs scores in [0, 1] (Eq. 1), and the Search agent outputs scores in [0, 1] (Eq. 2). The merged score S_merged = α·S_AI + β·S_KG + γ·S_Search (Section 3.7) linearly combines these without normalization. The case studies confirm this: for Topotecan–TOP1, AI=7.65, KG=1.0, Search=0.27, yielding a final score of 11.51. The AI score dominates by construction. The weights α, β, γ are described only as "weighting parameters" with details relegated to the stripped appendix, making it impossible to assess whether the multi-agent integration is anything more than the AI agent's prediction with cosmetic additions. (Sections 3.7, 4.2)

